The movement materialized during the polar night of 2006 inside an abandoned maritime research outpost outside Ny-Ålesund, Svalbard. A splinter collective of Scandinavian indietronica bootleggers and seasonal cryobiologists discovered that running cracked copies of FruityLoops through battery-powered Roland SP-404 samplers inside subterranean permafrost chambers warped the playback clock into uncanny, slow-motion cadences. Subculture adherents—calling themselves "Weavers"—congregate in sub-zero geodesic shelters, clad in floor-length, thrifted Icelandic wool cardigans layered over reflective mountaineering salopettes. Their live rituals involve "brumation circles," in which audiences lie motionless beneath thermal emergency blankets while performers use silver slide whistles, circuit-bent iPod Minis, and contact mics frozen into blocks of river ice to weave a fragile, heat-conserving cocoon of sound.

